flgreet -- the FreeLFS login screen
===================================

inittab runs /usr/sbin/flgreet on tty1 instead of agetty.

  flgreet     (root) takes tty1 as its controlling terminal and runs
              `flcomp --firstboot` with FLCOMP_SETUP=/usr/libexec/flgreet-ui,
              XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/flgreet and a clean environment. It reads
              one line, the user name, from a pipe. It accepts the name only
              if it is in /etc/passwd with a uid from 1000 to 59999, creates
              /run/user/UID (0700, owned by the user), and becomes
              `login -f NAME`. When login ends, init starts flgreet again.
              If flcomp cannot start, or crashes, it execs /sbin/agetty on
              tty1, so a broken desktop never locks you out of the console.
              Ctrl-Alt-F2 .. F6 still give plain console logins.
  flgreet-ui  (root, inside that compositor) the screen: accounts, password
              field, Log in, Shut down (a second click within 4 s confirms).
              The password is checked with crypt_r against /etc/shadow: an
              empty hash logs in without one, a hash starting with ! or *
              is locked, a wrong password waits one second. The password is
              never printed or logged, and its buffer is wiped after use.

Look: the card of flsetup, theme from /etc/freelfs/desktop.conf when that
file exists (theme=dark|light), else dark. The title is the boot screen's
"FreeLFS" wordmark at rest: right after flsplash handed over (/run/flsplash.released)
the first frames draw it where and how flsplash left it and ease it into the card
(640 ms, the card fades in); see docs/M7-DESIGN.md "Login screen take-over".
FLGREET_ANIM_T=MS (tests) shows that animation frozen at MS ms.

Build: make (flgreet needs no libraries; flgreet-ui needs flui and libcrypt).
